---
scope: src/tools/cleanup
alignment: unreviewed
description: Guarded eraser that removes a closed module's pipeline ephemerals only when each file it would remove is recorded completed or abandoned, refusing with the items still open.
intent: >
  cleanup is the eraser that pairs with scaffold's pen. When a module's
  cycle closes, its plan, todo list, and brief have served their
  purpose and go, leaving code and intent specs as the durable record. A
  raw shell delete is blocked by safety tooling and enforces nothing, so
  this tool holds the safety rules in code: it removes ephemerals only when
  every file it would remove records its cycle closed, completed with every
  item checked or abandoned, and otherwise refuses with exactly what
  remains. It reads statuses and never sets one. A closed todo list needs
  no plan beside it.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Given a module, the tool removes only that module's plan, todo list, and brief. Intent specs, research specs, code, the session log, and every other file survive every call untouched.
    - Removal proceeds only when every file it would remove is recorded completed, with every item re-checked and really checked, or recorded abandoned. A file from before statuses existed that records none but has every item checked is also removable, so nothing is stranded.
    - A missing todo list never blocks, and where no plan exists a closed todo list is still removed, so one written beside code planned in another module is never stranded. A brief is removed only together with its plan, and an abandoned plan takes its brief with it so discarded work can start over cleanly.
    - Every precondition, including that the path is an existing folder inside the project holding ephemerals, is settled before anything is removed, so a call cleans completely or removes nothing. A refusal names each unchecked item by file and line, so the caller knows exactly what work remains.
    - Because the real call is already safe, an agent closing a module calls it directly with no preview step. An opt-in dry run exists for inspection only, changes nothing, and never disagrees with what the real call would do against the same state.
    - Cleanup is a single cross-platform action a user can permit by name, so closing a module never requires a raw shell delete or a broad delete permission.
  undesired:
    - A call that removes, rewrites, or even reads the session log, an intent spec, a research spec, code, or any file that is not a pipeline ephemeral of the named module, however the module was named. The session log stays even when recorded abandoned, and no call serves as a listing of what a folder holds.
    - Removal of any plan, brief, or todo list still proposed or approved, a file recorded completed while an item is still open, or a brief whose plan is absent. Equally, a refusal that only says it refused, without naming each unchecked item and the file holding it.
    - A closed todo list, or a fully checked file from before statuses existed, refused only because no plan sits beside it or no status is recorded, leaving a manual shell delete as the only way out.
    - A half-cleaned module, with some ephemerals removed and others left behind, or a call against a path outside the project, a missing folder, or a folder with no ephemerals that reports success.
    - A dry run that predicts a different result than the real call, or a cleanup flow that treats a dry run as a required step before the real call.
    - A status the tool decides on its own, by setting or changing it, or by treating a file as completed or abandoned because of its age, its emptiness, or the caller asking, instead of the status already recorded in the file.
---
